// TERMINAL VELOCITY — LIVE WIRE — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// TERMINAL VELOCITY as a festival banger — THE BANGER of the three neon remixes. A
// game alternate of neon, selectable from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the
// desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// Hard-dance at 150: four on the floor, an off-beat bass under a pumping supersaw, and
// the hook on keys and bells rather than a saw — the neon cabinet's alloy chime, a
// bright pop grand in octaves under it, and Peter's resonant square pluck (roundMono2
// from his HARVEST edit) doubling it in the drops.
// 
// A DOUBLE DROP either side of neon-1's lightning. Seseragi drops first in C major at
// bar 9 — the whole six-bar phrase, bright. On beat three of bar 14 the band stops and
// the chime sweeps down in minor (neon's own outro sweep), and on BEAT 56 — the
// downbeat of bar 15, NEON_MINOR_TURN_BEAT, where neon-1's strike and its day-to-night
// change fire — the night drops: the same figure flipped to C minor. Drop two lands on
// bar 45, beat 176, the second strike in NEON_STRIKE_BEATS, a whole step up.
// 
// The cabinet screen plays bars 1–2 once and loops 3–6, as neon's own does, and the
// level picks the song up where the screen leaves it — so the strike lands 8–11 bars
// (13–18 s) into neon-1, TERMINAL VELOCITY's own lead time. That matters: a train whose
// flight starts before the turn only flies past, and neon-1's first boardable train
// (progress 0.30, about 26 s in) must find the night already fallen.
// 
//   1–4    intro — the chime's figure over strings
//   5–6    groove — kick, clap, piano off-beats (the cabinet screen loops bars 3–6)
//   7–8    build — snare eighths to sixteenths, riser, and a supersaw whose lowpass
//          opens across the two bars (every build has one)
//   9–14   DAY DROP — C major, the whole Seseragi phrase; stop on bar 14 beat 3
//   15–30  NIGHT DROP (beat 56) — the minor figure (8), then the second theme with
//          brass stabs and the Tron ostinato on the square pluck (8)
//   31–38  breakdown — the Shibuya melody on the piano, then the chime over it
//   39–44  build — Shibuya over the Tron bass; its E♭-minor half again as the lift
//   45–60  DROP TWO (beat 176) — D minor: ice bell 8va over the chime, brass every
//          bar, a second kick, the Tron sixteenths throughout
//   61–64  rebuild — B♭, C (the day's figure for one bar), A♭, G; loops to bar 15
// 
// The melody is TERMINAL VELOCITY's own, note for note: the Seseragi figure major and
// minor, its cadence, the second theme (neon bars 29–36), the Tron ostinato, the
// Shibuya melody and the opening sweep, which here is the pickup into every drop.
// Seseragi is JR East's Yamanote departure melody and the Shibuya melody is JR East's
// too — the clearing caveat on neon.js carries over to this file unchanged.
// 
// 64 bars at 150. Written by work/local/_neon-remix-live-wire.mjs through
// work/local/_remix-lib.mjs; re-running it rewrites this file.
//
// SPOT FX (1 Oct 2026, work/local/_spot-fx-stops.mjs): the stop on bar 14 beat 3 cuts every lane but the chime's run (lead4) and the riser, which lead into the strike.
//
// SPOT FX — LIGHTNING (1 Oct 2026, work/local/_spot-fx-picture.mjs): a sixteenth of the whole mix
